window.docContents[49].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/1-dimensional-representations-are-automatically-irreducible/#Chapter2___Remark2___3___13":{"contents":"Remark 2.3.13. Note that a 1-dimensional representation of any algebra is automatically irreducible.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u00091-dimensional representations are automatically irreducible","header":"1-dimensional representations are automatically irreducible","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/1-dimensional-representations-are-automatically-irreducible/#Chapter2___Remark2___3___13"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Central-character-of-representations/#Chapter2___Problem2___3___16___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Central character of representations\u0009Central character of representations","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Central-character-of-representations/#Chapter2___Problem2___3___16___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Hilbert-series-of-graded-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Hilbert-series-of-graded-algebras":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers","header":"Hilbert series of graded algebras","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Hilbert-series-of-graded-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Hilbert-series-of-graded-algebras"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results--Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: Matrix.single\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3/Derived12; role=supporting\n\nDeclaration: Pi.module'\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3/Derived6; role=supporting\n\nThe displayed coordinate modules over the auxiliary algebra are simple, exhaust its finite-dimensional simple modules, and every module over it is semisimple.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Introduction_to_3.3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3/Derived17; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.6.2/Derived12; role=supporting]\n\nEvery finite-dimensional module over the displayed algebra is equivalent to a direct sum of finite families of coordinate-vector modules.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.3.1; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3/Derived17; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.3.4/Derived8; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.3.1; role=primary]\n\nA product-ring linear equivalence between indexed auxiliary types at the same index induces a linear equivalence over the selected factor.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=supporting]\n\nA product-ring linear equivalence between nontrivial indexed auxiliary types forces their indices to agree.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn indexed auxiliary type is simple over the product ring exactly when its base type is simple over the selected factor.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=supporting]\n\nA linear equivalence over one factor induces a product-ring linear equivalence between the corresponding indexed auxiliary types.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=supporting]\n\nThe module structure over one factor on the range of the corresponding indexed auxiliary endomorphism.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=supporting]\n\nA simple module over a finite product of rings is linearly equivalent to an indexed auxiliary type formed from a simple range of an indexed auxiliary endomorphism.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary family of linear endomorphisms of a product-ring module, indexed by the factors.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=supporting]\n\nA module over a finite product of rings is simple exactly when one indexed auxiliary endomorphism range is simple over its factor and every other such range is zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.3. Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras\u0009Alternative proof of Theorem 3.3.1 without earlier results\u0009Alternative proof of Theorem 3.3.1 without earlier results\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results--Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Radicals-of-k_LSQ_x_RSQ_____LPAR_x___n_RPAR_-and-upper-triangular-matrices/#Chapter3___Example3___5___6___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.5. Finite dimensional algebras\u0009Radicals of k[x]/(x^n) and upper triangular matrices\u0009Radicals of k[x]/(x^n) and upper triangular matrices","header":"Formalization","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Radicals-of-k_LSQ_x_RSQ_____LPAR_x___n_RPAR_-and-upper-triangular-matrices/#Chapter3___Example3___5___6___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Proof-of-Theorem-5___23___2___-complete-reducibility-via-algebraic-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____--Proof-of-Theorem-5___23___2___-complete-reducibility-via-algebraic-representations":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.23. Algebraic representations of $GL(V)$","header":"Proof of Theorem 5.23.2: complete reducibility via algebraic representations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Proof-of-Theorem-5___23___2___-complete-reducibility-via-algebraic-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____--Proof-of-Theorem-5___23___2___-complete-reducibility-via-algebraic-representations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___7___-Virtual-representations/Section-5___7___-Virtual-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___7___-Virtual-representations--Section-5___7___-Virtual-representations":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.7. Virtual representations","header":"Section 5.7: Virtual representations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___7___-Virtual-representations/Section-5___7___-Virtual-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___7___-Virtual-representations--Section-5___7___-Virtual-representations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Composition-of-functors-and-identity-functor/#Chapter7___Discussion_after_Definition7___2___1":{"contents":"Note that functors can be composed in an obvious way. Also, any category has the identity functor.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.2. Functors\u0009Composition of functors and identity functor","header":"Composition of functors and identity functor","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Composition-of-functors-and-identity-functor/#Chapter7___Discussion_after_Definition7___2___1"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Properties-of-projective-dimension/#Chapter9___Problem9___4___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.4. Homological dimension\u0009Properties of projective dimension\u0009Properties of projective dimension","header":"Formalization","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Properties-of-projective-dimension/#Chapter9___Problem9___4___2___formalization"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___7___-Morita-equivalence--Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories--Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Shows that two candidates satisfying the stated pair of shared conditions are algebra equivalent.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=supporting]\n\nShows that two candidates satisfying the other stated pair of shared conditions are algebra equivalent.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=supporting]\n\nBounds the rank of a candidate by that of the ambient algebra under the other stated conditions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=supporting]\n\nThe displayed relation holds for a field paired with itself.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=supporting]\n\nThe relation holds when both arguments are the same ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=supporting]\n\nReverses an instance of the relation between two rings.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=supporting]\n\nComposes two compatible instances of the relation between rings.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.7. Morita equivalence\u0009Uniqueness of basic algebra for finite abelian categories\u0009Uniqueness of basic algebra for finite abelian categories\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___7___-Morita-equivalence--Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories--Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});